# NanoKVM SD card partition layout for Sophgo SG2002 / LicheeRV Nano
#
# Partition map (MBR, all four primaries):
#   raw:  512 KiB    - U-Boot environment, BOTH redundant copies, 64 KiB each
#                        at 0x80000 and 0x90000. This is the ONLY mutable boot
#                        state and it lives in the all-zero 1 MiB gap between
#                        the MBR and p1, outside every filesystem: a redundant
#                        env is two copies switched atomically, so a power cut
#                        mid-write always leaves one valid. It carries the RAUC
#                        slot state (BOOT_ORDER, BOOT_<slot>_LEFT).
#   p1: FAT16,  80 MB  - fip.bin (OpenSBI+U-Boot FIT), boot.scr, boot_a.itb /
#                        boot_b.itb (each a single FIT: kernel + DTB +
#                        initramfs), and the NanoKVM runtime config files
#                        (IMAGE_BOOT_FILES). MOUNTED READ-ONLY at runtime --
#                        only nanokvm-update remounts it, to write the inactive
#                        slot's FIT, and it puts it back.
#   p2: 512 MB         - rootfs slot A: the squashfs-zst image (read-only;
#                        the initramfs lays a volatile tmpfs overlay over it).
#   p3: 512 MB         - rootfs slot B: empty ext4 placeholder at factory.
#                        nanokvm-update writes a new squashfs here and moves
#                        slot b to the head of BOOT_ORDER; U-Boot's RAUC
#                        bootmeth passes the slot to the initramfs as
#                        rauc.slot= on the cmdline, and the initramfs falls
#                        back to the other slot when the selected one does not
#                        mount.
#   p4: 640 MB         - ext4 userdata partition (/var/lib/nanokvm). EMPTY at
#                        factory. It used to ship pre-populated with a Pi boot
#                        image for the USB mass-storage gadget to serve; the
#                        BMC does not store host firmware any more (host
#                        firmware is updated with FMP capsules), so there is
#                        nothing to seed and no build-time image surgery.
#                        This is the only persistent writable zone: media,
#                        firmware/capsule staging, logs and application state
#                        all live here, reached through symlinks from the
#                        read-only rootfs. It is grown to fill the card by a
#                        userspace one-shot (nanokvm-growdata) AFTER boot,
#                        deliberately not by the initramfs: rewriting the
#                        partition table of the disk you are booting from is
#                        the one operation that can make the card unbootable,
#                        and it has no business on the boot path.
#
# This is a .wks.in template: ${...} bitbake variables are expanded before
# wic runs. rawcopy accepts the absolute IMGDEPLOYDIR path; the squashfs-zst
# artifact/symlink exists by then via IMAGE_TYPEDEP:wic in nanokvm-image.bb.
#
# NOTE: each wic .wks directive must be on a single line - the parser
# shlex-splits each line individually and does not support backslash
# continuations (a trailing '\' raises "No escaped character").
#
# --ondisk must be "mmcblk0" (not "mmcblk"): wic derives the /etc/fstab device
# name from it, so "mmcblk" yields "/dev/mmcblkp1" (a non-existent node) and the
# /boot mount silently fails. "mmcblk0" yields the correct "/dev/mmcblk0p1".

bootloader --ptable msdos
# U-Boot environment: both 64 KiB copies, 0x80000..0xA0000, written as one
# 128 KiB blob (nanokvm-uboot-env concatenates the copy with itself).
# --no-table keeps it out of the MBR: these are raw sectors, not a partition.
# It is clear of p1 (1 MiB) and of the ~43 KiB the boot ROM reads from the head
# of the card.
#
# Both copies are seeded on purpose. Writing only the primary leaves the second
# slot as a hole in the .wic, and `bmaptool copy` -- what the machine conf
# recommends and hack/flash-sd.sh prefers -- skips holes. Re-flashing a card
# that had ever run saveenv would then keep its OLD redundant copy, and both
# U-Boot and libubootenv pick whichever copy has the higher flag byte: the
# stale one. Two identical copies are fine; with equal flags both pick the
# primary.
#
# UNITS: --offset defaults to KiB but --fixed-size defaults to MiB, so the
# suffix on 128K is load-bearing. Without it wic reserves 128 MiB here and
# shoves every following partition out by that much (p1 landed at sector
# 133120 instead of 2048). 128K covers both env copies so nothing else can be
# allocated into the redundant slot.
part --source rawcopy --sourceparams="file=${DEPLOY_DIR_IMAGE}/uboot-env.bin" --no-table --offset 512K --fixed-size 128K
part /boot --source bootimg-partition --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype msdos --label BOOT --active --align 1024 --size 81920K
# --fstype on a rawcopy part never runs mkfs (content comes from the file);
# it only sets the MBR partition type (ext4 -> 0x83 instead of the vfat
# default's 0xc).
part --source rawcopy --sourceparams="file=${IMGDEPLOYDIR}/${IMAGE_LINK_NAME}.squashfs-zst" --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype ext4 --align 1024 --fixed-size 524288K
part --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype ext4 --label rootfsB --align 1024 --fixed-size 524288K
# 640M initial size: empty ext4 plus headroom. The partition entry and
# filesystem are grown to the full card after boot by nanokvm-growdata, so this
# size only decides how many bytes a flash writes.
part --ondisk mmcblk0 --fstype ext4 --label nkvm-data --align 1024 --fixed-size 640M
